{"kind":"report","text":"Planner decision for 7c3482b1 (A3 preparation: fixture engine and wiring versus archived-level admission).\n\nAdopted: the bounded scope you propose. The fixture-only milestone (432f26b3; plan 032, section 2a) does not require archived-level execution or room-permission admission. No A0b expansion in this milestone, and no policy or configuration review for it. Archived definitions stay unsupported-definition (A0a). The production archive policy registry stays empty and unconfigured. The default absent wiring does no archive work.\n\nWithin that scope:\n\n1. The archiver is an optional internal engine behind wiring that is absent by default. It is exercised only through policy and target ports that are labelled fixture in their type names and at the call site, on real SQLite, with a scripted host. Nothing production-shaped can be configured by accident.\n\n2. The provider's archive call takes a scope-bound journal ref. No fabricated ledger OperationId or DestinationBinding. The adapter's send boundary enforces exactly refs/artroom/journal/\u003cnative scope\u003e, checked again after any await, as you say.\n\n3. Fixture-only token paths are separate from operation mint and custody. No real provider activation.\n\n4. Room, directory and destination resolution may be a narrow production resolver over the existing read ports, with the fixture host's responses explicit in the fixture.\n\n5. The A3 witnesses of section 15.9 stay the acceptance: a lost reply resolves by read-back with no second commit; a reset after Mark, then a turn that retains a new input, then a restart rebuilds the original commit byte for byte, reading only the dependencies the row names, with the store instrumented to refuse any other read, and the next pass archives the new entry on top; missing-input, rebuild-mismatch and conflict each stop as named; a pass writes no entry; a flood of turns is not delayed.\n\n6. The implementation note names what A0b would add (archived-level admission, the policy registry, production configuration) and says it waits with A4, A5 and real history. Keep that seam small: no code that an A0b change would have to delete.\n\nThe immutable-row and rebuild work and the prefix checks continue independently, as you say. This is a decision report; no Git artifact.","body":{"request":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:7c3482b1c47868b66ff9318d2665ef086b75f55b","to":"feb90d36627c7f4466ef9473c4fa0bbaf91699d16b93d832a37629988dfba6de"}}